 or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ . Objective Zinc deficiency is common in patients with liver cirrhosis, and the effects of zinc supplementation on cirrhosis remain controversial. This study aims to explore the effectiveness of zinc supplementation in the clinical treatment of cirrhosis and hepatic encephalopathy. Methods MEDLINE, Embase, Cochrane Library, Scopus, Ovid, and Web of Science databases were searched with a cutoff date of March 2025. A meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials on the efficacy of zinc supplementation in the treatment of liver cirrhosis and hepatic encephalopathy was performed using RevMan5.4 and StataMP17 software. Results Seven studies comprising 338 patients with cirrhosis (172 patients in the treatment group and 166 patients in the control group) were included. Results indicated that zinc supplementation slightly increased serum albumin levels compared with those of the control group (WMD=0.07, 95% CI [0.00, 0.14], P=0.04). In particular, zinc supplementation dose of <50 mg and time of ≥16 weeks showed a slight advantage. Zinc supplementation also improved（alanine aminotransferase）ALT content (WMD=−8.83, 95% CI [−15.47, −2.19], P =0.009), reduced blood ammonia levels (WMD=−5.92, 95% CI [−10.52, −1.32], P =0.01), and shortened the NCT - A time (WMD = 7.31, 95% CI [-9.52, -5.09], P < 0.001). Conclusion Zinc supplementation may modestly improve nutritional parameters, hepatic function, and reduce the risk of hepatic encephalopathy in patients with cirrhosis. However, some outcomes demonstrated limited clinical significance, and further research is needed to determine the optimal dosage and duration of zinc supplementation. Keywords Cirrhosis Zinc deficiency Zinc supplement Hepatic encephalopathy Xing'an League Science and Technology Program Project MBJH2024001 Yang Zhenni pmc-status-qastatus 0 pmc-status-live yes pmc-status-embargo no pmc-status-released yes pmc-prop-open-access yes pmc-prop-olf no pmc-prop-manuscript no pmc-prop-legally-suppressed no pmc-prop-has-pdf yes pmc-prop-has-supplement no pmc-prop-pdf-only no pmc-prop-suppress-copyright no pmc-prop-is-real-version no pmc-prop-is-scanned-article no pmc-prop-preprint no pmc-prop-in-epmc yes pmc-license-ref CC BY-NC-ND issue-copyright-statement © BioMed Central Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2026 Introduction Cirrhosis is a progressive chronic liver disease induced by various causes and characterized by diffuse necrosis, insufficient hepatocyte regeneration, angiogenesis, severe fibrosis, and pseudolobule formation. Malnutrition is a remarkably burden for patients with cirrhosis, affecting 20% of patients with compensated cirrhosis and over 50% of those with decompensated liver disease [ 1 , 2 ]. Malnourished patients are at an increased risk of zinc deficiency. The liver is a central organ in zinc metabolism, and various liver diseases can lead to zinc deficiency. Conversely, zinc deficiency can alter hepatocyte function and immune responses. Therefore, abnormal zinc levels are closely associated with the occurrence and progression of liver diseases, particularly cirrhosis. Normal serum zinc levels range from 70 mcg/dL to 150 mcg/dL. Zinc deficiency is diagnosed when serum zinc levels are below 70 mcg/dL (< 12 mmol/L). This condition can be hereditary or acquired. Acquired zinc deficiency is commonly caused by insufficient zinc intake, malabsorption, increased demand, or excessive loss. Hereditary zinc deficiency is mainly associated with mutations in the SLC39A4 gene, which encodes ZIP4, leading to a triad of symptoms, namely, alopecia, diarrhea, and dermatitis, also known as acrodermatitis enteropathica [ 3 ]. Several studies suggested that zinc supplementation may improve liver function, nutritional status, and prevent complications such as ascites and hepatic encephalopathy (HE) in patients with cirrhosis [ 4 , 5 ]. Moreover, low-dose zinc supplementation has been shown to prevent the deterioration of clinical states in cirrhosis [ 4 ]. However, a few reports suggested that zinc supplementation fails to elevate and may even reduce serum albumin levels [ 6 ]. Zinc concentration is decreased in patients with cirrhosis, and zinc is implicated in HE pathogenesis. However, the impact of zinc supplementation on mental performance remains controversial [ 7 – 9 ]. A meta-analysis found that zinc supplementation combined with lactulose over 3–6 months improved the number connection test-A (NCT-A) in patients with cirrhosis and HE [ 10 ]. However, a double-blind clinical trial reported the minimal effects of zinc supplementation on HE [ 7 ]. Studies investigating zinc replacement in HE are limited, and the treatment outcomes are inconsistent. The relationship between zinc and cirrhosis progression and complications remains contentious. The potential role of zinc supplementation as an adjunct to current standard therapies for HE, such as lactulose and rifaximin, is supported by its distinct mechanism of action. While lactulose and rifaximin primarily target the gut to reduce ammonia generation and absorption, zinc serves as an essential cofactor for urea cycle enzymes (e.g., ornithine transcarbamylase) and for glutamine synthetase in skeletal muscle, thereby enhancing ammonia clearance via alternative pathways [ 11 – 13 ]. This theoretical synergy suggests that zinc could complement the efficacy of existing treatments. However, the clinical evidence synthesizing this combined effect remains inconclusive. A retrospective US study, the addition of zinc to standard therapy (lactulose and rifaximin) failed to demonstrate a further reduction in readmission rates among patients with HE [ 14 ]. This suggests that the real-world synergy of the triple therapy (zinc, lactulose, and rifaximin) is more complex than theoretically anticipated, warranting further in-depth exploration. Although previous meta-analyses have investigated the role of zinc supplementation in cirrhosis and its complications [ 10 , 15 ], these studies have primarily focused on specific neuropsychological test outcomes in HE [ 10 ] or hard clinical endpoints such as mortality [ 15 ]. However, evidence regarding the comprehensive impact of zinc supplementation on a broader range of nutritional parameters, biochemical markers of hepatic function, and the risk of hepatic encephalopathy in patients with cirrhosis remains inconsistent and has not been fully elucidated. Therefore, this study aims to conduct an updated meta-analysis to more comprehensively evaluate the effects of zinc supplementation on nutritional parameters, biochemical markers of hepatic function, and the risk of hepatic encephalopathy in patients with cirrhosis, based on the existing body of evidence, and to further explore its potential clinical implications. Methods This meta-analysis was conducted in accordance with the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions and was written following the PRISMA guidelines. The study protocol has been registered with the PROSPERO International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (registration number: CRD42024558611, University of York, UK). Search strategy A single investigator (YiFan Gong) searched the following six databases: MEDLINE [via PubMed], Embase, Cochrane Library, Scopus, Ovid, and Web of Science. The search was not restricted by date, age, gender, or language. The search period spanned from database inception to March 19, 2025, with results updated before conducting the pooled analysis. The search strategy comprised three main components combined using the AND operator. Each component included MeSH terms and free-text terms, which were linked using the OR operator. The initial search strategy was developed for PubMed and then adapted for the other five databases using equivalent terms: Intervention drugs (“Zinc”[Mesh], Zinc Isotopes, Zinc Radioisotopes); Relevant diseases (“Liver”[Mesh], Livers); Study design (randomized controlled trial [RCT], randomized, placebo). Full search strings: ("Zinc"[Mesh]) OR ((Zinc Isotopes [Title/Abstract]) OR (Zinc Radioisotopes [Title/Abstract]))) AND (("Liver"[Mesh]) OR (Livers [Title/Abstract]))) AND (randomized controlled trial [Publication Type] OR randomized [Title/Abstract] OR placebo [Title/Abstract] To minimize bias, we also searched clinical trial registries such as ClinicalTrials.gov and ProQuest Dissertations for gray literature. However, no additional unpublished studies meeting our inclusion criteria were identified. The reference lists of retrieved articles were also manually checked to identify any potentially missed studies. Study selection Endnote 20 was applied for literature screening and management. Two researchers (YiFan Gong and XuQian Zhang) independently screened the retrieved articles. Titles and abstracts were initially reviewed to exclude studies not meeting the inclusion criteria. Full texts of potentially eligible studies were further reviewed to confirm eligibility. Disagreements were resolved through discussion or adjudication with the third researcher (XiaoYan Xue or Guofu Sun ). Studies meeting the following criteria were included: 1.Study design: Published RCTs with no language restrictions. Participants: Patients with cirrhosis caused by chronic hepatitis, alcohol-related liver disease, nonalcoholic liver disease, or other etiologies. No restrictions were placed on patient gender, age, ethnicity, region, disease duration, or sample size. Intervention: Zinc supplementation in the experimental group (Z group) compared with conventional treatment in the control group (S group). Conventional treatment entailed liver protection, lactulose, and nutritional support, with the exclusion of zinc-containing drugs. Outcomes: Studies must report at least one of the following extractable outcomes: blood ammonia, serum albumin, alanine aminotransferase (ALT), total bilirubin, Digit Symbol Test A, or Child–Pugh score. Changes in these outcomes after a minimum of 6 weeks of treatment were required. The exclusion criteria were as follows: RCTs without a control group. Animal studies or other nonhuman trials. Studies involving patients with liver diseases other than cirrhosis. Studies where the control group received zinc-containing medications. Studies with inaccessible full texts. Studies with outcome variables that could not be extracted (e.g., outcomes presented solely in graphical format). Data extraction Data were extracted in accordance with the Cochrane Collaboration standards and included the following information: Basic study information: First author, year of publication, and journal. Key elements of bias risk assessment. Baseline characteristics of study participants: Gender, age, sample size, etiologies of cirrhosis, zinc supplementation dose, and follow-up duration. Interventions and outcomes: Blood ammonia, serum albumin, alanine aminotransferase (ALT), total bilirubin, Digit Symbol Test A, and Child–Pugh score. Two researchers (YiFan Gong and XuQian Zhang) independently extracted the data. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion. If the outcome data were presented in graphical form, Getdata v2.26 software was used to extract numerical values, which were then calculated using appropriate formulas. Risk of bias assessment The quality of the included studies was evaluated using the Cochrane Risk of Bias Tool recommended in the Cochrane Handbook (version 5.2.2). A bias risk summary was plotted using StataMP 17 software, with focus on the following: Whether the randomization method was appropriate. Implementation of allocation concealment. Blinding of participants and personnel. Blinding of outcome assessors. Completeness of outcome data. Selective reporting of outcomes. Other potential biases (e.g., small sample sizes and baseline imbalance). Two researchers (YiFan Gong and XuQian Zhang) independently rated the quality of each study. Disagreements were resolved through discussion with the third researcher (Zhenni Yang). Statistical methods Meta-analyses, including heterogeneity testing, meta-analysis, publication bias analysis, and sensitivity analysis, were performed using RevMan 5.4 and StataMP 17 software. Continuous variables were expressed as weighted mean differences (WMDs) with corresponding 95% confidence intervals (CIs). Heterogeneity Testing: Heterogeneity among the studies was evaluated using chi-square test and quantified using the I² statistic. According to the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions, I² > 50% suggests substantial heterogeneity. Given the small sample size and potential heterogeneity, a random-effects model was employed to account for unexplained variability. Subgroup Analysis: Regardless of significant heterogeneity, subgroup analyses according to the characteristics of the included studies were conducted to explore the effects of potential confounding factors. Publication Bias: For analyses with five or more studies, funnel plots were used to assess publication bias. Begg’s test was applied for the quantitative evaluation of bias. Sensitivity Analysis: Sensitivity analyses were conducted to assess the robustness of the results.A P -value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. A P -value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results Literature search results A total of 1751 articles were retrieved from the databases. After duplicate removal, 952 articles remained. Initial screening excluded systematic reviews, meta-analyses, and animal studies, leaving 728 articles. Following the exclusion of studies with irrelevant content, 36 articles were deemed potentially eligible for full-text review. Further exclusions were made for studies with mismatched interventions or outcome variables and inaccessible full texts. Finally, seven RCTs were included in the analysis [ 4 – 6 , 8 , 9 , 16 , 17 ]. The literature screening process and results are summarized in the PRISMA-compliant flowchart created using GraphPad Prism 9.5.1 (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1 Flowchart of Study Inclusion Characteristics of included studies and assessment of risk of bias The basic characteristics of the included studies are summarized in Table 1 . Seven RCTs [ 4 – 6 , 8 , 9 , 16 , 17 ] involving 338 patients were included, with 172 patients in the experimental group (Z group) and 166 patients in the control group (S group). Table 1 Characteristics of the 7 Studies Included in This Meta-Analysis Source Age, mean (SD), y Gender (M/F) Causes of cirrhosis cases follow-up time, m Basal level of blood zinc, Mean (SD), mcg/dl Dose of zinc supplementation in group Z, mg/d Group Z Group S Group Z Group S Group Z Group S Miho Hayashi,2007 [ 5 ] Group Z : 66.0 (9.9)Group S : 65.1 (11.3) 10/9 13/8 HBV/HBC/other 19 21 6 58.4 (9.2) 60.2 (9.0) 45 Rattaya Janyajirawong,2021 [ 16 ] Group Z : 60.06 (8.1)Group S : 57.74 (9.1) 29/6 27/7 HBV/HBC/other 35 34 3 68.47 (20.6) 68.65 (16.6) 45 Nasser Mousa,2016 [ 17 ] Group Z : 54.5 (9.6)Group S : 55.8 (9.2) 17/14 15/12 HBV/HBC/other 31 27 3 49.6 (11.2) 46.9 (10.5) 25 Y. Takuma,2010 [ 8 ] Group Z : 66.5 (5.7)Group S : 66.5 (7.4) 17/22 22/16 HBV/HBC/other 39 38 6 48.9 (9.3) 51.6 (13.3) 51 Mohammad Hossein Somi MD,2012 [ 4 ] Group Z : 51.30 (15.80)Group S : 44.00 (15.20) 19/6 16/11 Nonalcoholic cirrhosis 25 27 3 63.97 (4.2) 67.42 (3.02) 11.25 K. Weismann,1979 [ 6 ] Group Z : 56 (10.75)Group S : 59 (3.25) - - alcoholic cirrhosis 16 14 1.5 86.45 (10.71) 81.25 (15.28) 135 Kazuhiro Katayama,2014 [ 9 ] Group Z : 64.3 (7.1)Group S : 73.6 (8.4) 3/4 4/1 Cirrhotic patients with hyperammonemia 7 5 3 55.1 (8.11) 51.8 (8.37) 150 Abbreviations : SD Standard deviation, M/F Male/Female, Group Z Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention), Group S Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention), HBV Hepatitis B