e, treatment duration, and age. The results were as follows: Fig. 4 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of albumin. Abbreviations: SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) 1. Zinc dosage: (1) zinc dosage < 50 mg: WMD = 0.08, 95%CI (0.00, 0.15), P = 0.04 < 0.05, I2 = 0%, indicating that albumin levels were higher in the experimental group than in the control group after treatment; and (2) zinc dosage ≥ 50 mg: WMD = 0.02, 95%CI (− 0.16,0.20), P = 0.83 > 0.05, I2 = 0%, showing no statistically significant difference (Fig. 4 A). Treatment duration: (1) treatment duration < 16 weeks: combined effect size = 0.00, confidence interval 95%CI (− 0.12,0.13), with no statistical significance z = 0.03 (P = 0.98 > 0.05); and (2) treatment duration ≥ 16 weeks: combined effect size = 0.10, confidence interval 95% CI (0.02, 0.18), with statistical significance z = 2.39 (P = 0.02 < 0.05), indicating that albumin levels in the experimental group were 0.10 higher than those in the control group after treatment for ≥ 16 weeks (Fig. 4 B). Age: (1) age < 60 years: combined effect size = 0.01, confidence interval 95% CI (− 0.13, 0.16), with no statistical significance z = 0.19 (P = 0.85 > 0.05); and (2) age ≥ 60 years: 95%CI (0.01, 0.16), with statistical significance z = 2.18(P = 0.03 < 0.05), indicating that the albumin levels in the experimental group were 0.09 higher than those in the control group after treatment (Fig. 4 C). Effect of zinc supplementation on blood ammonia levels Five RCTs [ 5 , 8 , 9 , 16 , 17 ] comprising 256 patients were ultimately selected from the seven eligible studies. Meta-analysis results showed that the blood ammonia levels in the experimental group were lower than those in the control group (WMD = − 5.92, 95% CI [− 10.52, − 1.32], P = 0.01, I 2 = 0%), with statistically significant difference, suggesting that the intervention was effective (Fig. 5 ). The data were divided into two groups according to zinc dosage: group with zinc dosage < 50 mg (WMD = − 5.01, 95%CI [− 9.75, − 0.28], P = 0.04, I 2 = 0%) and group with zinc dosage ≥ 50 mg (WMD = − 21.29, 95%CI [− 40.74, − 1.84], P = 0.03, I 2 = 0%). Both groups showed statistically significant reductions in blood ammonia levels compared with the control group. In particular, the group receiving ≥ 50 mg zinc demonstrated a significantly greater reduction in blood ammonia levels compared with that receiving < 50 mg zinc (Fig. 5 A). When grouped by treatment duration, the results for treatment duration < 16 weeks (WMD = − 7.20, 95%CI [− 15.71, 1.30], P = 0.10 > 0.05, I 2 = 0%) and ≥ 16 weeks (WMD = − 9.92, 95%CI [− 25.94, 6.10], P = 0.22 > 0.05, I 2 = 60%) were not statistically significant (Fig. 5 B). Fig. 5 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of ammonia. Abbreviations: SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) Effect of zinc supplementation on ALT levels Four RCTs [ 6 , 9 , 11 , 12 ] involving 169 patients were ultimately selected from the seven eligible studies. The combined effect size was (WMD = − 8.83, 95% CI [− 15.47, − 2.19], P = 0.009, I 2 = 0%), indicating that the ALT levels in the experimental group were 8.83 units lower than those in the control group after treatment (Fig. 6 ). When subgroup analysis based on zinc dosage was conducted, the results showed that for zinc doses < 50 mg (WMD = − 11.68, 95%CI [− 19.39, − 3.97], P = 0.003, I 2 = 0%), the ALT levels in the experimental group were significantly lower than those in the control group. However, for zinc doses ≥ 50 mg (WMD = − 0.62, 95%CI [− 13.71,12.47], P = 0.93, I 2 = 0%), the results were not statistically significant (Fig. 6 A). Subgroup analysis by age showed that the results for patients aged < 60 years (WMD = − 7.57,95% CI [− 16.27, 1.14], P = 0.09, I 2 = 10%) and ≥ 60 years (WMD = − 10.81,95% CI [− 25.47, 3.85], P = 0.15, I 2 = 26%) were not statistically significant (Fig. 6 B). Fig. 6 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of ALT. Abbreviations: ALT, alanine transaminase;SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) Effect of zinc supplementation on NCT-A Three RCTs [ 8 , 11 , 12 ] involving 204 patients were ultimately selected from the seven eligible studies. Meta-analysis results showed that the experimental group performed significantly better than the control group (WMD = − 7.31,95% CI [− 9.52, − 5.09], P < 0.001, I 2 = 0%) (Fig. 7 ). This finding indicates that the time required to complete NCT-A was shorter in the experimental group than in the control group. Fig. 7 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of NCT-A. Abbreviations: NCT-A,number connection test part A;SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) Effect of zinc supplementation on bilirubin Three RCTs [ 6 , 8 , 12 ] involving 165 patients were selected from the seven eligible studies for the analysis of zinc supplementation efficacy. Meta-analysis results showed that the bilirubin levels in the experimental group were lower than those in the control group (WMD = − 0.06, 95% CI [− 0.29, 0.17], P = 0.60, I 2 = 32%), but the difference was not statistically significant (Fig. 8 ). Fig. 8 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of bilirubin. Abbreviations: SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) Effect of zinc supplementation on Child–Pugh scores Three RCTs [ 4 , 8 , 11 ] involving 198 patients were ultimately selected from the seven eligible studies. Meta-analysis results indicated that the Child–Pugh scores in the experimental group were lower than those in the control group (WMD = − 0.35, 95% CI [− 0.76, 0.06], P = 0.09), but the difference was not statistically significant (Fig. 9 ). Fig. 9 Results of meta-analysis and subgroup analysis of Child-Pugh(s). Abbreviations: SD,Standard deviation;Group Z, Experimental group (patients were treated with zinc supplement drug intervention); Group S,Control group (patients received conventional drug intervention) Sensitivity analysis and bias assessment Sensitivity analyses were conducted for the four groups of studies (albumin, ammonia, ALT, NCT-A, bilirubin and Child–Pugh scores), and no single study had a strong influence on the related results. Egger’s test results for the four groups were as follows: albumin group P = 0.176, ammonia group P = 0.057, ALT group P = 0.604, NCT-A group P = 0.568, and bilirubin group P = 0.69 and Child–Pugh scores group P = 0.568, indicating no significant publication bias in these data groups. Discussion This study strictly followed the inclusion criteria, incorporating seven studies and enrolling 338 patients with liver cirrhosis (172 in the treatment group and 166 in the control group). The analysis focused on albumin, ammonia, ALT, bilirubin, Child–Pugh scores, and NCT-A. Other clinical indicators with insufficient data were not included. Our findings indicate that Zinc supplementation modestly improves biochemical parameters and neuropsychometric performance in patients with cirrhosis, but the clinical significance remains uncertain. Subgroup analysis revealed that zinc supplementation of < 50 mg, treatment duration ≥ 16 weeks, and age ≥ 60 years were associated with slight improvements in albumin levels. Zinc supplementation ≥ 50 mg showed a pronounced reduction in ammonia levels, whereas extended treatment duration did not significantly reduce ammonia levels. In addition, zinc supplementation < 50 mg reduced ALT levels, whereas ≥ 50 mg supplementation did not. We also found no significant effect of zinc supplementation on Child–Pugh scores, suggesting that zinc therapy may not reverse the progression of cirrhosis but rather play a protective role by slowing disease progression. Current research on zinc supplementation in patients with cirrhosis revealed inconsistent results. Up to 83% of these patients experience zinc deficiency, which is also associated with disease severity and survival rates [ 18 ]. Miho Hayashi et al. [ 5 ] reported that low-dose zinc supplementation prevents the deterioration of clinical status in patients with cirrhosis, whereas others argued that zinc supplementation is unrelated to clinical outcomes in liver cirrhosis [ 19 ]. To clarify the effects of zinc on cirrhosis, we conducted a meta-analysis of RCTs on zinc supplementation for cirrhosis up to March 2025. Overall, our results suggest that the influences of zinc supplementation on liver function, nutritional status, and HE in patients with cirrhosis are modest, and modulated by dosage, duration, and patient age. Over 75% of patients with decompensated cirrhosis exhibit hypoalbuminemia and zinc deficiency [ 20 ]. Zinc deficiency in patients with cirrhosis may result from decreased albumin synthesis, altered zinc transport, reduced zinc absorption in the intestines, increased urinary zinc excretion, or diuretic-induced suppression of renal tubular zinc reabsorption [ 21 – 25 ]. Vansh Deep et al. [ 26 ] also found a significant positive correlation between serum albumin and zinc levels. Compared with the control group, zinc supplementation improved the serum albumin levels (WMD = 0.07, 95% CI [0.00, 0.14], P = 0.04). Subgroup analysis also showed that low-dose zinc (< 50 mg) improved serum albumin and ALT levels in patients with cirrhosis, and zinc supplementation for ≥ 16 weeks significantly improved serum albumin levels (WMD = 0.10, 95% CI [0.02, 0.18], P = 0.02). These findings provide valuable insights for exploring the optimal dosage and treatment duration of zinc supplementation. This meta-analysis revealed that zinc supplementation can increase serum albumin levels, suggesting a statistically significant but clinically marginal difference. This magnitude of change may fall below the predefined threshold for minimal clinically important difference and thus fail to translate into clinical benefits for this patient population. To date, evidence remains unclear regarding the optimal serum albumin threshold associated with clinical improvements in cirrhosis. The ANSWER study by Caraceni et al. [ 27 , 28 ] demonstrated an 80% reduction in all-cause mortality (HR = 0.20, 95% CI [0.08–0.48]) in patients with ascites achieving serum albumin ≥ 40.0 g/L after 1 month of intensive albumin therapy. Meanwhile, the ATTIRE randomized trial [ 29 ] showed no significant improvement in composite endpoints (infection, renal dysfunction, or mortality) (RR = 1.05, 95% CI [0.91–1.21]) among patients with decompensated cirrhosis and albumin levels maintained at ≥ 30.0 g/L, suggesting the controversial mortality implications of albumin elevation. Therefore, the influence of zinc-induced albumin modulation on mortality or other clinical outcomes requires further investigation through well-designed trials targeting relevant thresholds. The latest cross-sectional study found that hypozincemia is significantly related to the mortality rate in HE [ 30 ]. Several studies also found a negative correlation between serum zinc levels and HE grading [ 26 , 31 ]. Early research [ 12 , 32 ] has proven that zinc supplementation can significantly reduce blood ammonia concentrations. Horiguchi et al. [ 33 ] and Miwa et al. [ 34 ] also reported the positive effects of zinc supplementation on the overall condition of patients with HE. These findings are consistent with our study, which found that zinc supplementation modestly reduced ammonia levels. Subgroup analysis revealed that high doses of zinc (≥ 50 mg) significantly reduced ammonia levels compared with low doses (< 50 mg), suggesting that the former has a slightly greater impact on blood ammonia levels. However, Riggio et al. [ 35 ] did not find that zinc supplementation improved HE in a double-blind, crossover trial of short-term (10 day) oral zinc supplements. The short duration of zinc supplementation in this study might not have been sufficient to accurately assess its true impact on HE. Zinc plays a critical role in ammonia detoxification. A negative correlation between serum zinc levels and blood ammonia levels has been observed in patients with cirrhosis [ 36 ]. Zinc deficiency may impair ammonia-reduction pathways, leading to elevated ammonia levels [ 8 ]. Low zinc status regulates nitrogen metabolism by reducing the activity of hepatic urea cycle enzymes and glutamine synthetase in muscles [ 11 , 37 ]. Zinc supplementation may prevent HE by activating ornithine transcarbamylase. Minimal HE (MHE) is a form of neurocognitive dysfunction with a prevalence of approximately 60%–80% in patients with cirrhosis, but its clinical diagnosis can be challenging [ 38 , 39 ]. NCT-A is a test of visual–spatial orientation and psychomotor speed that helps in the early detection of latent MHE. Studies found that antioxidant combined with zinc therapy can improve NCT-A performance in patients with MHE and can lower ammonia levels, and after continuous treatment for 3 months [ 17 ], suggesting that the combined antiinflammatory and antioxidant effects significantly improve biochemical and psychological test outcomes and offer substantial protective effects for patients with cirrhosis and MHE. Takao et al. [ 34 ] demonstrated that zinc deficiency in patients with cirrhosis and MHE is a predictor for the development of overt HE and mortality. Our research included three RCTs comprising 204 patients and found that zinc supplementation treatment could significantly shorten the NCT-A time (WMD = − 7.31, 95% CI [− 9.52, − 5.09], P < 0.00001). However, the current evidence remains inconclusive regarding the optimal dosing regimen and duration of zinc supplementation because of the limited number and small sample sizes of RCTs. Future large-scale RCTs with rigorous methodology are warranted to establish evidence-based zinc supplementation strategies. Correcting zinc deficiency can have a significant impact on human health [ 40 ], especially for patients with cirrhosis where zinc supplementation can exert positive effects through multiple mechanisms.Oral zinc supplements are the primary treatment for zinc deficiency. Zinc sulfate was found to reverse alcohol-induced steatosis in male mice by suppressing oxidative stress and reactivating hepatocyte nuclear factor-4α and PPAR-α [ 19 ]. Polaprezinc, a complex of zinc and l-carnosine, exhibits antiinflammatory effects by reducing liver iron stores [ 41 ]. Furthermore, zinc supplementation prevents increases in transaminase levels, reduces liver fibrosis, and lowers triglyceride levels [ 42 , 43 ]. Some studies suggested that high zinc levels can decrease glucose production, enhance glucose uptake, reduce lipid deposition, and ameliorate high-fat-diet-induced liver damage [ 44 ]. However, this finding contradicts our study’s subgroup analysis, which concluded that high zinc doses (≥ 50 mg) did not reduce ALT levels. Zinc deficiency is known to manifest as growth retardation, diarrhea, hair loss, glossitis, weakened immunity, and hypogonadism [ 45 ]. By contrast, excessive zinc supplementation poses risks of chronic zinc toxicity [ 46 ], leading to immune suppression, reduced HDL cholesterol levels, anemia, and copper deficiency-related complications. Therefore, the efficacy and safety of high-dose zinc supplementation for improving liver function in patients with cirrhosis warrant further investigation. In this study, zinc supplementation showed no significant effect on bilirubin levels or Child–Pugh scores in patients with cirrhosis. This phenomenon may be due to the inclusion of only three RCTs with small sample sizes, leading to insufficient statistical power. Therefore, these results should be interpreted with caution. Underlying liver disease etiology may differentially influence zinc metabolism through distinct pathophysiological pathways, potentially leading to varied responses to zinc supplementation [ 47 , 48 ]. Unfortunately, the lack of individual-level data precluded subgroup analyses by etiology. Furthermore, substantial variability existed in zinc formulations and dosages. The preparations used (zinc sulfate, acetate, and polaprezinc) differ in elemental zinc content and bioavailability, which may partly explain the inconsistent findings across studies [ 15 ]. Limitations This study has several limitations. First, the small number of eligible studies ( n = 7) and limited total sample size preclude definitive conclusions, particularly regarding long-term outcomes such as mortality and HE recurrence. Substantial heterogeneity in baseline zinc levels and dosing regimens across studies, coupled with insufficient stratification of cirrhosis etiology and severity, may have affected the assessment of the treatment effect. Furthermore, potential bias could arise from data conversion methods and the relatively short follow-up durations. Despite these limitations, our analysis provides a quantitative synthesis of evidence supporting zinc’s role as an adjunctive therapy in cirrhosis, by showing its modestly increasing albumin levels and the biological plausibility of its involvement in ammonia metabolism. Conclusion Zinc plays an important role in cirrhosis treatment. Zinc deficiency should be assessed in patients with cirrhosis, particularly those with HE. Zinc supplementation may slow the progression of cirrhosis, providing new clinical strategies for managing this condition. However, the efficacy, optimal dosing, and duration of zinc supplementation require further validation through high-quality RCTs. Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Yifan Gong, Zhenni Yang and Guofu Sun contributed equally to this work. Authors’ contributions Yifan Gong and Zhenni Yang contributed to the design and developed methodology. Yifan Gong, Guofu Sun and Xiaoyan Xue contributed to the interpretation and analysis of data.Yifan Gong wrote the manuscript. Xuqian Zhang has revised the manuscript. Funding The authors would like to thank the Xing’an League Science and Technology Program Project(MBJH2024001)for financially supporting this study. Data availability The datasets used and/or analysed during the current study available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Not applicable. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. References 1. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on nutrition in chronic liver disease J Hepatol 2019 70 1 172 93 30144956 10.1016/j.jhep.2018.06.024 PMC6657019 EASL Clinical Practice. Guidelines on nutrition in chronic liver disease. J Hepatol. 2019;70(1):172–93. 30144956 10.1016/j.jhep.2018.06.024 PMC6657019 2. Nutritional status in cirrhosis Italian multicentre cooperative project on nutrition in liver cirrhosis J Hepatol 1994 21 3 317 25 7836699 Nutritional status in cirrhosis. Italian multicentre cooperative project on nutrition in liver cirrhosis. J Hepatol. 1994;21(3):317–25. 7836699 3. Stiles LI Ferrao K Mehta KJ Role of zinc in health and disease Clin Exp Med 2024 24 1 38 38367035 10.1007/s10238-024-01302-6 PMC10874324 Stiles LI, Ferrao K, Mehta KJ. Role of zinc in health and disease. Clin Exp Med. 2024;24(1):38. 38367035 10.1007/s10238-024-01302-6 PMC10874324 4. Somi MH Rezaeifar P Ostad Rahimi A Effects of low dose zinc sup